The DeviceTree error `lacks #forward-cells` on node `/i2c@400/target@20` (which is labeled `i2c1`) indicates that the DTS parser expects this node to define how many cells follow its phandle when it's referenced in a `phandle-array` property.

In the `&i2c0` node, the `forwards` property is defined as `forwards = <&i2c1 0x20>, <&i2c2 0x24>;`. This is a `phandle-array` where `&i2c1` is a phandle to the `target@20` node, and `0x20` is a single cell (the I2C address) associated with that phandle.

For a node to be correctly referenced in a `phandle-array` property, it must define a `#<property-name>-cells` property to specify how many cells it consumes. In this case, the property name is `forwards`, but the error message explicitly states `#forward-cells` (singular). Regardless of the naming discrepancy, the intent is to specify the number of cells. Since one cell (`0x20`) follows the `&i2c1` phandle, the `i2c1` target node needs to declare `#forward-cells = <1>`. The same applies to `i2c2`.

This fix adds the `#forward-cells = <1>` property to both `i2c1` and `i2c2` target nodes, satisfying the DTS parser's requirement.

